Person-Based Similarity Index . The personalised estimates of interindividual variability were investigated using the PBSI, calculated according to the formula below, for each SA, CT and SV pro /uniFB01 les [34 -37]. The process for calculating the PBSI scores begins with concatenating the respective regional measures into vectors that represent the pro /uniFB01 le of each speci /uniFB01 c brain phenotype; PBSI-SA, PBSI-CT and PBSI-SV, respectively. This produces a simpli /uniFB01 ed, personalised index for each phenotypic neuroanatomical pro /uniFB01 le. This index can then be used in one of two ways; /uniFB01 rst, to quantify how similar an individuals ' brain pro /uniFB01 le is to that of other individuals with the same clinical pro /uniFB01 le or disorder (within-diagnosis or within-group). Second, to quantify how similar an individuals ' brain pro /uniFB01 le is respective to a normative estimate, i.e. the average of the healthy control group (normativeness) [37].

